{"name":"Stone Lion","hex":"#b3a491","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"stone lion","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Synonym of foo dog"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/stone_lion"}],"entries":[{"title":"Chinese guardian lions","blocks":[{"text":"Chinese guardian lions, or imperial guardian lions, are a traditional Chinese architectural ornament. Typically made of stone, they are also known as stone lions or shishi. They are known in colloquial English as lion dogs, foo dogs, or fu dogs. The concept, which originated and became popular in Chinese Buddhism, features a pair of Asiatic lions — often one male with a ball that represents the material elements and one female with a cub that represents the element of spirit — that were thought to protect the building from harmful spiritual influences and harmful people that might be a threat. Used in imperial Chinese palaces and tombs, the lions subsequently spread to other parts of Asia including Japan, Okinawa, Korea, Mongolia, the Philippines, Tibet, Thailand, Myanmar, Vietnam, Sri Lanka, India, Nepal, Cambodia, Laos, Singapore, and Malaysia."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Transmongolie-660_%2827643744552%29.jpg/330px-Transmongolie-660_%2827643744552%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_guardian_lions","wikidata":"Q1069772"},{"title":"Rock (geology)","blocks":[{"text":"In geology, a rock is any naturally occurring solid mass or aggregate of minerals or mineraloid matter. It is categorized by the minerals included, its chemical composition, and the way in which it is formed. Rocks form the Earth's outer solid layer, the crust, and most of its interior, except for the liquid outer core and pockets of magma in the asthenosphere. The study of rocks involves multiple subdisciplines of geology, including petrology and mineralogy. It may be limited to rocks found on Earth, or it may include planetary geology that studies the rocks of other celestial objects."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Marsh_Butte_and_Geikie_Peak%2C_Grand_Canyon.jpg/330px-Marsh_Butte_and_Geikie_Peak%2C_Grand_Canyon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rock_(geology)","wikidata":"Q8063"},{"title":"Lion","blocks":[{"text":"The lion is a large cat of the genus Panthera, currently ranging only in Sub-Saharan Africa and India. It has a muscular, broad-chested body; a short, rounded head; round ears; and a dark, hairy tuft at the tip of its tail. It is sexually dimorphic; adult male lions are larger than females and have a prominent mane that extends from the head to the shoulders and chest."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/020_The_lion_king_Snyggve_in_the_Serengeti_National_Park_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg/330px-020_The_lion_king_Snyggve_in_the_Serengeti_National_Park_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lion","wikidata":"Q140"},{"title":"Lion (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Lion is a color that is a representation of the average color of the fur of a lion."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/020_The_lion_king_Snyggve_in_the_Serengeti_National_Park_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg/330px-020_The_lion_king_Snyggve_in_the_Serengeti_National_Park_Photo_by_Giles_Laurent.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lion_(color)","wikidata":"Q6555379"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Forbidden_City_Imperial_Guardian_Lions.jpg/500px-Forbidden_City_Imperial_Guardian_Lions.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Forbidden_City_Imperial_Guardian_Lions.jpg/500px-Forbidden_City_Imperial_Guardian_Lions.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AForbidden_City_Imperial_Guardian_Lions.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/The_entrance_of_the_Chinese_Museum%2C_Melbourne.jpg/500px-The_entrance_of_the_Chinese_Museum%2C_Melbourne.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/The_entrance_of_the_Chinese_Museum%2C_Melbourne.jpg/1280px-The_entrance_of_the_Chinese_Museum%2C_Melbourne.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/The_entrance_of_the_Chinese_Museum%2C_Melbourne.jpg/500px-The_entrance_of_the_Chinese_Museum%2C_Melbourne.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Guardian lions outside the Chinese Museum in Melbourne. In accordance with feng shui, the male lion, with the ball under his right paw, is on the right, and the female, with the cub under her left paw, is on the left.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_entrance_of_the_Chinese_Museum%2C_Melbourne.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Cina_settentrionale_o_centrale%2C_grande_leone_guardiano_di_tomba%2C_dinastia_han_orientale%2C_I-III_sec..JPG/500px-Cina_settentrionale_o_centrale%2C_grande_leone_guardiano_di_tomba%2C_dinastia_han_orientale%2C_I-III_sec..JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Cina_settentrionale_o_centrale%2C_grande_leone_guardiano_di_tomba%2C_dinastia_han_orientale%2C_I-III_sec..JPG/1280px-Cina_settentrionale_o_centrale%2C_grande_leone_guardiano_di_tomba%2C_dinastia_han_orientale%2C_I-III_sec..JPG?u